# !! DO NOT TYPE A REAL VALUE INTO THIS FILE. It is committed and this repository is
# !! PUBLIC. The placeholders below are deliberately not blank, so that a value left
# !! unreplaced fails loudly at authentication instead of silently becoming an empty
# !! credential. Copy this file first; edit only the copy.
#
# TEMPLATE — committed. Copy it, do not edit it in place:
#
#   cp apps/production/email-stalwart/env.secret.stalwart.example \
#      apps/production/email-stalwart/.env.secret.stalwart
#   $EDITOR apps/production/email-stalwart/.env.secret.stalwart
#   ./scripts/encrypt-env-files.sh apps/production/email-stalwart/.env.secret.stalwart
#
# All from the REPO ROOT — the script resolves its arguments against your current
# directory. It takes a single file (as above, encrypting only that one) or a directory
# (encrypting every .env* file under it, which also rewrites the other .encrypted files
# in this directory — harmless, but it makes for a noisier diff).
#
# Run that last command YOURSELF rather than asking the agent to. The agent must never
# create, read or write the real path: on 2026-08-29 two credentials were burned because
# the real file was in the agent's read-set and the harness reported the change including
# the secret line, even though the edit happened outside the agent's tools. The fix is
# structural — the agent writes the template, a human makes the copy.
#
# The copy is gitignored (`.env*`); only the `.encrypted` output is committed.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS FILE IS A TEMPLATE, NOT A BACKUP
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# If .env.secret.stalwart already exists with real values, DO NOT copy over it. Edit the
# existing copy in place and re-run the encrypt script. The three values below are not
# independently re-derivable — STALWART_RECOVERY_ADMIN and STALWART_SMARTHOST_PASSWORD
# must match state that lives outside this repo (the Stalwart datastore and Google
# respectively), so overwriting them locks the server out of both.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Recovery admin, as `user:password` in one value, e.g. STALWART_RECOVERY_ADMIN=admin:s0mep4ssword
#
# The fallback administrator Stalwart accepts when the datastore has no usable account —
# this is what you log in to the admin UI with, not the one-time password printed at first
# start (README "First login"). NOT randomly generated here: the same credential is split
# across .env.secret.stalwart-bootstrap (STALWART_BOOTSTRAP_USER / _PASSWORD) and the two
# MUST hold the same value. If they drift nothing breaks until the next rebuild, which then
# fails at its first step.
STALWART_RECOVERY_ADMIN=<REPLACE-ME-user:password>

# Password for the Google SMTP relay account named by MtaRoute route-google-relay
# (authUsername scott@willeke.com) in stalwart-config/plan.ndjson. NOT generated here —
# it is a Google app password, minted at https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords for the
# relay account. Google prints it with spaces; strip them.
STALWART_SMARTHOST_PASSWORD=<REPLACE-ME-google-app-password>

# Basic-auth password for Stalwart's Prometheus exporter at /metrics/prometheus. Username
# is `prometheus`, fixed in the Metrics singleton in stalwart-config/plan.ndjson.
#
# Generate with:  openssl rand -base64 24
#
# A plain shared secret compared verbatim, not a hash — unlike the Traefik basic-auth
# credentials elsewhere in this repo, which are bcrypt via `htpasswd`. Paste the base64
# output as-is: no surrounding quotes (secretGenerator keeps quote characters literally as
# part of the value) and no leading or trailing whitespace. Prometheus applies
# strings.TrimSpace to its password_file while Stalwart's env var is exact, so whitespace
# on one side only produces a 401 that looks like a wrong password. Base64's alphabet
# (A-Za-z0-9+/=) needs no escaping through dotenv, SOPS, or the HTTP Basic header.
#
# THE SAME VALUE MUST BE WRITTEN TO A SECOND FILE, in another namespace and so another
# Secret:
#
#   apps/production/monitoring/prometheus/.env.secret.stalwart-metrics
#   key: stalwart_metrics_password       (lowercase — it becomes the mounted filename)
#
# Rotate both together, in one push. The two sides pick the new value up differently and
# a brief disagreement is normal:
#
#   - Stalwart: `stalwart-creds` is generated WITH a name suffix hash, so a new value
#     renames the Secret, changes the StatefulSet pod template, and Flux restarts the pod.
#     The restart is required either way — Stalwart resolves authSecret once at
#     config-build time and ignores a changed Secret until it restarts.
#   - Prometheus: `stalwart-metrics-auth` sets disableNameSuffixHash, so the Secret is
#     updated in place and the kubelet refreshes the mounted file within about a minute,
#     with no restart.
#
# So expect StalwartMetricsScrapeDown to fire transiently during a rotation, and check the
# status code before treating it as a real fault: 401 is drift between these two files,
# 404 is the exporter being off in the datastore (README "Metrics endpoint returns 404,
# or returns 401").
#
# Do not leave this unset hoping it fails safe. If Stalwart cannot resolve the variable it
# degrades the endpoint to UNAUTHENTICATED rather than erroring, and that endpoint shares
# an HTTP listener with the internet-published admin UI. stalwart-metrics-ingress.yaml
# blocks the path at the edge as an independent second layer for exactly this reason.
STALWART_METRICS_PASSWORD=<REPLACE-ME-openssl-rand-base64-24>
